ir\n1059\tINJURED: where did he get it? Where\n1060\tdid he get it?' and when his ceremonial\n1061\tobligations were discharged he departed\n1062\tstill repeating increasingly: Where did\n1063\the get it? Where did he get it2'\n1064\t\n1065\t\n1066\tTraffic managers of great lines have\n1067\tbeen observed to prosper in ratio\n1068\twholly disproportionate to their salaries\n1069\tIn fact. very many OF them have become\n1070\texceedingly rich, matter which has\n1071\tnot altogether escaped criticism. Some\n1072\tof them have made money by going\n1073\tlong or short Of the stock OF the cor\n1074\tporation employing them, accordingly\n1075\tas their information and Judgment\n1076\tprompted and IN respect of THIS practice\n1077\tthe public censuro has never been severe\n1078\t1t is not unlikely that the course which\n1079\tMr.. ROOSEVELT has pursued toward the\n1080\tmanagement OF tine railroads may pro-\n1081\twoke a more minute and attentive scru-\n1082\ttinv of the practices of their officials,\n1083\tin which case it may appear that the\n1084\tsources of their wealth were far more\n1085\tquestionable.\n1086\t\n1087\t\n1088\tWe venture to think that Mr.. RoosE-\n1089\tWELT * BY this time satisfied that the\n1090\treally grave charge against the rail-\n1091\troads of the country is not the high\n1092\trates charged for transportation but\n1093\tthe giving of secret and criminal re-\n1094\tbates We have never INDUCED the\n1095\tbelief that rate cutting was inspired\n1096\tsolely by desire for increased tonnage.\n1097\tThe rebates and cut rates enjoyed by\n1098\tthe Standard Oil Company cannot be\n1099\texplained solely by the competition\n1100\tbetween rival roads for the oil COM\n1101\tpany's traffic. No one who is conversant\n1102\twith the railroad business could be hood\n1103\twinked into such a belief. There were\n1104\tcertain phenomena connected with the\n1105\tgrowth of that company which are,\n1106\tindeed. inscparableSfr the history Of\n1107\tall great corporations during the last\n1108\tquarter OF a century, but which have\n1109\tescaped general notice and have never\n1110\tbeen brought out into the light.\n1111\t\n1112\t\n1113\tIf President ROOSEVELT persists in\n1114\tthe direction which has led to the late\n1115\trevelations touching the criminal man\n1116\tagement of the Atchison, Topeka and\n1117\tSanta Fe Railroad, some of the phenom-\n1118\tena will see the light beyond a doubt and\n1119\tif. in consequence, some Of our leading\n1120\trailroad men find themselves in the penl-\n1121\tTertiary they will have only themselves\n1122\t10 blame, and the public may fifty con-\n1123\tgraduate itself. Then and in that\n1124\tevent there will no longer remain any\n1125\tillusions Us to the nature and causes Of\n1126\trailroad acknowledgement, and they will\n1127\tbe found to have been at least as pref\n1128\tjudicial to the interests Of the people who\n1129\tactually own the railroads as they have\n1130\tbeen detrimental TO The public welfare.\n1131\tThe profit and advantage of the secret\n1132\trebate have not accrued solely 10 the\n1133\tCORPORATION for whose benefit it was\n1134\tapparently created.\n1135\t\n1136\t\n1137\tThe late Mr. JAMES AICHENRY often\n1138\tcomplained 10 US that what he described\n1139\tas ghost trains were run upon the Erie\n1140\tRailroad, trains which performed their\n1141\tdue function upon the operating sched-\n1142\tules OF the road, but of which the results\n1143\tnever penetrated into the traffic returns.\n1144\tHad he lived longer and become ac-\n1145\touainted with these later revelations of\n1146\tthe methods Of railroad management\n1147\twe believe he would have hit upon a\n1148\tmore practical and adequate hypothesis.\n1149\tThere are two sides to the question, and\n1150\tboth are of grave Importance; and the\n1151\tplundering of the public has proceeded\n1152\tpar passu with the defraudng Of the\n1153\trailroad investor We are informed that\n1154\tone railroad which freed itself from the\n1155\trate cutting and rebate system showed\n1156\tan increase of earnings IN the first twelve\n1157\tmonths of its emancipation of something\n1158\tlike SIC. 600. $50 Certain Industries which\n1159\twere involved were less profitably con-\n1160\tQuoted. perhaps. while ertainindividuals\n1161\tconcerned found their personal emolp-\n1162\tments subjected 10 a severe shrinkage.\n1163\t\n1164\t\n1165\tWe hope the Atchison scandal will be-\n1166\turobed TO its uttermost depths and that\n1167\tthe President will let no guilty mar\n1168\tescape. We could wish that the duty\n1169\tof doing the necessary work dcvolvec\n1170\tupon a body Of better repute thar\n1171\tthe Interstate Commerce Commission\n1172\twhich for incompetence and peryersitJ\n1173\tis without a peer and which enjoys\n1174\tin an equal degree the contempt ot\n1175\tthe courts and the indifference Of thu\n1176\trailroads Perhaps, too, the President\n1177\twill be convinced that in the pursuit\n1178\tof his amb ition he has found a weapon\n1179\tmore effective than Congress and less\n1180\tdoubtful than an appeal from the Con\n1181\tstitution. We mean public opinion. II,\n1182\thas certainly enlisted it as it never wa.\n1183\tenlisted before, and we think that viii\n1184\tits aid anything that is desirable can b'\n1185\taccomplished. 1t were better far thai\n1186\tthe public continued to suffer for a time\n1187\tand that railroad owners were robbed %\n1188\tall they possess than that the principle\n1189\tand spirit of our theory OF govcramen\n1190\t\n1191\t\n1192\t5000 fro,, Vur., ,-reDus lun\n1193\t\n1194\t=== 1_1896-07-06_p14_sn85066387_0017503741A_1896070601_0113.json\n1195\tThe San Francisco call. · 1896-07-06\n1196\tcrops: None\n1197\theadline: She Wants to Join Her Husband\n1198\tin the Spirit Land When\n1199\t\n1200\t\n1201\tUnder Them.\n1202\t\n1203\tIS NOW IN A PADDED CELL\n1204\t\n1205\tThe Doctors at the Receiving Hos.\n1206\tpital Can See No Signs of In.\n1207\t\n1208\t\n1209\tsanity in Her.\n1210\t---\n1211\tThe case of Mrs. Allie Gender, widow\n1212\t53 years of age, will form subject for in\n1213\tvestigation by the Insanity Commission\n1214\ters this morning.\n1215\t\n1216\t\n1217\tShe is in padded cell in the Receiving\n1218\tHospital charged with insanity, but any\n1219\tone who talks with her cannot see the\n1220\tslightest sign OF mental alteration in hel\n1221\tconduct or speech. Yet her friends say\n1222\tshe is crazy, and too dangerous to be al\n1223\tlarge.\n1224\t\n1225\t\n1226\tTwelve years ago her husband was killed\n1227\tin railroad accident. He had an accl-\n1228\tdent policy for $500, and this sum was\n1229\tpaid to the widow. or rather to Albert\n1230\tShainwald, her brother-in-law, living at\n1231\t207 Geary street, who was administrator Of\n1232\ther husbands estate.\n1233\t\n1234\t\n1235\tThe widow was left with three young\n1236\tchildren, boy and two girls. They were\n1237\tput in home and the mother worked\n1238\thard to support herself and them. The\n1239\t$500 was placed on deposit in the San\n1240\tFrancisco Savings Union bank and with\n1241\tinterest it has reached the sum of $2100. OF\n1242\tthis amount $500 has been withdrawn and\n1243\tloaned to G. Petrie, printer in Alameda.\n1244\t\n1245\t\n1246\tMrs. Gender about two years ago went\n1247\tto live with her brother-in-law, but three\n1248\tweeks ago she removed to 238 Sixth street,\n1249\twhere she engaged a room. According TO\n1250\tthe landlady, Mrs. George, she showed\n1251\tsymptoms of insanity shortly after being\n1252\tin her house and on Saturday she became\n1253\tviolent and threatened to take her life, so\n1254\tDR.. Bohm was sent for and he ordered\n1255\tthat she be sent to the Receiving Hospital.\n1256\t\n1257\t\n1258\tHer chief hallucination is that her hus-\n1259\tbands spirit is constantly with her.\n1260\tWhen walking on the street she imagined\n1261\that har hnos do J., II\n1262\tverse with him as if he were in the flesh\n1263\tat night she would alarm every one in\n1264\tthe house by going from room to room\n1265\tsearching for her husband and when she\n1266\tcould not find him she became violent and\n1267\thad to be placed under restraint.\n1268\t\n1269\t\n1270\tWhen she became calm she would talk\n1271\tnationally and say she wanted to Join her\n1272\thusband in the spirit land and if the\n1273\tmoney was only divided among her chill\n1274\tdren she would soon be with him.\n1275\t\n1276\t\n1277\tThe Oldest, young man % years of age,\n1278\tis in Mendocino County, where he has\n1279\tbought some Government land. One of\n1280\tthe girls is married and the other is a\n1281\tservant with family on Union street.\n1282\tApparently the money is not to be di-\n1283\tvided till the youngest. who is now 20\n1284\t| years of age. attains the age of 2i.\n1285\t| Speaking of her arrest Mrs. Gender said\n1286\tyesterday: sl think it is all a Joke to\n1287\tkeep me here. was in my room vester.\n1288\tday when two men wearing blue uniforms\n1289\tcame and asked me to put on my shoe.\n1290\tand come with them. Then they put me\n1291\tin patrol wagon and brought me here\n1292\t| But | don't know why. l am not crazy\n1293\tand | cannot see why l should be kept\n1294\there\"\n1295\t\n1296\t\n1297\tThe doctors at the hospital who have\n1298\tconversed with her say that she does not\n1299\texhibit the slightest symptoms of in.\n1300\tsanity, but the landlord and landlady o.\n1301\tthe lodging-house at 238 Sixth Street, DR.\n1302\tBohm and others say that she has spells\n1303\tand when under them she is violently\n1304\t/ insane.\n1305\t\n1306\t=== 30_1946-07-13_p2_sn83045462_00280604781_1946071301_0507.json\n1307\tEvening star. · 1946-07-13\n1308\tcrops: None\n1309\theadline: 4o Seek Ghost Job\n1310\t---\n1311\tPROVIDENCE, R. I. (P).-Just 40\n1312\tmen passed civil service examina-\n1313\tton for motor vehicle inspector.\n1314\tbut they're holding onto their pres\n1315\tent jobs-if they have any. For\n1316\tthere is not single vacancy for\n1317\tthe post.\n1318\t\n1319\t=== 6_1914-07-25_pna_sn86061215_00295875410_1914072501_0441.json\n1320\tOttumwa tri-weekly courier. [volume] (Ottumwa, Iowa) 1903-1916 · 1914-07-25\n1321\tcrops: None\n1322\theadline: ,cr1Aprrp xv-(Concluded.)\n1323\t---\n1324\tfCHApTER xv.-(Concluded)\n1325\t\n1326\t\n1327\tBut only for a minute; then they\n1328\tcaved. crumpled up, and took their\n1329\tmoney-perhaps because couple OF\n1330\tDAYS before they had been watching\n1331\tme tossing tomato cans into the ali\n1332\tnd,bouring them with my pistol, with\n1333\tfewer misses than looked good, to\n1334\tthem.\n1335\t\n1336\t\n1337\tThree months did fir the Double\n1338\tCircla. Pacif1ed, there was no further\n1339\tneed of my services. My happy little\n1340\touting was over, and it was trail back\n1341\tto the home: back, with the scent OF\n1342\tlife In my nostrils and the thrill of\n1343\taction in my blood, into the scent or\n1344\tthe grave and the asphyxiating atmos-\n1345\tphere Of the mentally feeble and mort\n1346\tbund.\n1347\t\n1348\t\n1349\tBut. happily, the Double Circla Incl\n1350\tdent proved the dawning OF the bright\n1351\test period Of my life, a dawning whose\n1352\tshining rays were shortly to lead me\n1353\tinto a harbor Of peace and Joy; OF con-\n1354\ttentment and bliss, that I had never\n1355\tdreamed could be mine.\n1356\t\n1357\t\n1358\tDown OF the home one day came\n1359\tfrom the redwood lumber camps OF\n1360\tnorth California an old soldier whom\n1361\t/ had known as a lad in Ionia County.\n1362\t\n1363\t\n1364\tThrough the war he had served IN\n1365\tMichigan regiment. Only twice since\n1366\thad he revisited the old home. but he\n1367\thad continued correspondence with his\n1368\tfamily.\n1369\t\n1370\t\n1371\tFrom him I learned-oh, Joy! the\n1372\tgreatest that could come to me-not\n1373\tonly that Tom Harper had recovered\n1374\tof the wound that had left me regard\n1375\tinR myself for half a century as an\n1376\toutlaw. not only that my mischievous\n1377\tBessie McVicker still lived, but that\n1378\tthrough all the weary years OF my ap'\n1379\tsence she had remained single!\n1380\t\n1381\t\n1382\tGould It be that she had withheld\n1383\ther heart and hand through all those\n1384\tyears for memory Of me? No. That\n1385\twere indeed folly Of the widest.\n1386\t\n1387\t\n1388\tsha wait for me? She, who had\n1389\tnever passed me so much as kindly\n1390\tword. who was ever making me the\n1391\tsubject Of cruel jests! She, above all,\n1392\twho had run to Toms side and taken\n1393\this head In her lap when, fallen under\n1394\tmy blow he lay so still In the snow!\n1395\t\n1396\t\n1397\tNonsense. Doubtless instead she\n1398\thad forgotten the fact had ever ex-\n1399\tlisted. But would know; must\n1400\tknow\n1401\t\n1402\t\n1403\tPromptly her answer came. Fol\n1404\tlowed a correspondence in which she\n1405\towned she had loved me from the first;\n1406\tthat she we'll deserved the sousing IN\n1407\tthe sap-trough had given her; and\n1408\tthat she had only run to Tom IN fear\n1409\tmy blow had killed him. and In the\n1410\thope she might revive him and save\n1411\tme the grave consequences that must\n1412\tensue in case he died at my hands.\n1413\t\n1414\t\n1415\tBut oh! the grief intermingled 4\n1416\tour 10y! She remained as poor as |;\n1417\tcould no more come to me than could\n1418\t70 to her She was shackled by her\n1419\tpoverty in the sugar-bush where had\n1420\tleft her, I by mine in the Soldiers\n1421\tHome. We were like two disembodied\n1422\tspirits reunited, denied the pressure OF\n1423\tdearly loved lips. the clasp Of quick\n1424\tpulsing hands.\n1425\t\n1426\t\n1427\tBut, ali! the pride and happiness our\n1428\tletter intercourse brought me! Bessie\n1429\thad stayed true to me as to her. And\n1430\ttome it was all the dearer that mine\n1431\thad been a silent alteration had never\n1432\tdared express, and yet so strong she\n1433\tmust have sensed it, to have responded\n1434\tq plainly she had, by lifetime's de-\n1435\tvotion.\n1436\t\n1437\t\n1438\tTime passed-much time-until we\n1439\t\n1440\t=== 1_1897-06-30_p4_sn82015104_00280768765_1897063001_0257.json\n1441\tThe record-union. · 1897-06-30\n1442\tcrops: None\n1443\theadline: FATHER AND SON.\n1444\t\n1445\tThey Are Having Trouble Over the\n1446\t\n1447\t\n1448\tLatter's Earnings.\n1449\t---\n1450\tWhat will probably prove an inter\n1451\testing case will be tried before a Po-\n1452\tlice Court jury next Saturday morn\n1453\ting.\n1454\t\n1455\t\n1456\tComplaint was made at police head\n1457\tquarters on Saturday last, by the\n1458\tfather and brother of Pearley Chap\n1459\tpell, that that young man had fallen\n1460\tunder the influence of an alleged spir-\n1461\titualist, named Mrs. Quarles, and at\n1462\ther suggestion had refused to return\n1463\tto the paternal roof\n1464\t\n1465\t\n1466\tThe boys story is that his father\n1467\tand brother interfered to prevent him\n1468\tfrom drawing his salary and that he\n1469\thas not been allowed fair share of\n1470\this earnings.\n1471\t\n1472\t\n1473\t1t was at first the intention of the\n1474\telder Chappell to have his son Pear\n1475\tley, who is about IS years old, ar\n1476\trested, but acting on subsequent ad-\n1477\tvice concluded to bring a charge of\n1478\tdisturbing the peace against Mrs.\n1479\tQuarles.\n1480\t\n1481\t\n1482\tYoung Chappell's brother, who is en-\n1483\tployed in Lavensons shoe store, has\n1484\talso caused the arrest of Mrs. Clough,\n1485\ta respectable woman who keeps a 1odg-\n1486\t"}]},"uuid":"7ce317b3-5b38-4989-b2d4-73518bfe9177","timestamp":"2026-10-31T07:34:30.564Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-hotragn-Documents-Claude-Code-HacktoberFest-week4-Paperghosts/1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b/scratchpad/c1/g01.txt","content":"=== 8_1883-04-21_p1_sn86076999_00415627385_1883042101_0867.json\nMorning appeal. [volume] (Carson City, Nev.) 1877-1906 · 1883-04-21\ncrops: ['/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/data/crops/9b46a1e5e158fc5c90fb2382.jpg', '/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/data/crops/6edd0fb35f212cf0fde8edd4.jpg', '/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/data/crops/983f1f80b5ddc8a749484139.jpg']\nheadline: A GHOST SAVES HIS CHAR.\n\n\nACTER\n\nA spiritual Conn unicatlon That\n\n\nDid some Good.\n---\nDid Some Good.\n\n\nA gentleman employed at ;\n\n\nGovernment institution in thi.\n\n\ncity, and a man who has nc\n\n\nleaning whatever toward spirit\n\n\nualism, relates the following in\n\n\ncident\n\n\nIn 1853 his father, while\n\n\ntreasurer of a local railroad in\n\n\nMassachusetts,, died. After his\n\n\ndemise the directors of the COM\n\n\npany found a deficiency in the\n\n\naccounts Of the deceased\n\n\namounting to eighteen hundred\n\n\nand fifty odd dollars. The fact\n\n\nwas communicated to the fam\nlily of the late treasurer and the\napparent detalcation caused\n\n\nthem much grief and shame.\nIn 1861 a noted Boston medium\n\n\nnamed Mansfield, visited San\nFrancisco, creating great ex-\ncitement by reason of his Won\nderful performances and he was\ncalled on by some Of the best\npeople of that city. One day\nthe gentleman above referred to,\naccompanied by the late Rev.\nThomas Starr King, visited\nMansfield purely out of curiosity.\nThe former was requested by\nthe medium to write upon a\nslip of paper the name of any\none in the spirit world with\nwhom he desired to communi-\ncate. He wrote the name of his\nfather, and without exhibiting\nil to Mansfield placed it into a\nsmall tin tube which the latter\nhanded him and sealed it in\nsuch a manner with a private\nseal that any tampering could\nbe easily detected. Mansfield\nrequested that the tube and the\ncontents be left in his possession\nfor twenty-four hours, after\nwhich the gentlemen might call\nfor a letter from the spirit world.\nOn the following day the gen\ntleman in question again called\non the medium and substan-\nitaly received this communi.\ncation from his deceased father\n\n\nHe informed his son that he\nwas cognizant of the grief which\nlis apparent defalcation had |\ncaused the family, but that he |\nwas entirely innocent of any !\nfurther stating that /\nf a certain book would be ex- |\n,mined, which could be found |\nn a stated spot, and opened at /\n/ given page, it would be found |\nhat the date of the year had\nenormously been placed in the\nlollar column and added up as\nash, which would account for\nhe apparent deficiency in his\nAlthough reposing\ntut little faith in the matter,\nhe son wrote East about it, and !\nwhen he received a reply it was\n@ the purport that the book\nmentioned was in the indicated\nlace, and on the stated page\nt was found thai 1858, the\nof the supposed defal-\nalton, had been added to the\nash account by mistake.\n\n=== 1_1893-06-24_pna_sn94052989_00175040250_1893062401_0653.json\nThe morning call. [volume] (San Francisco [Calif.]) 1878-1895 · 1893-06-24\ncrops: ['/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/data/crops/7011f58d3ce855c0c49db39f.jpg', '/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/data/crops/236d11faf74ed962becc363e.jpg', '/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/data/crops/fd74bee3529815e438295e8b.jpg', '/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/data/crops/f0abbc8e2cad9ca6cc338043.jpg', '/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts/data/crops/87ace9ab09c4c5fd48c4f4ae.jpg']\nheadline: WITHOUT ANY EXCUSE\n\nForging Documents to. Inculpate\n\n\nthe Statesmen of France.\n\nThe Ghost of Panama Will Not Down,\nThough 1t Should Be at\n\n\nRest.\n\nSpecial to THE MORNING CALL\n---\nLONDON, June 23.-The matter of the\ndocuments that the Paris Boulangist organ.\nCocarde. alleged have been stolen from\nthe British Embassy in Paris, came up in\nthe Commons to-day. Sir Edward Grey,\nParliamentary Secretary Of the Foreign\nOffice. replied that no documents had been\nstolen from the British embassy. He\nadded that the documents NJ. Millevoye\nread were gross torgeries, and be did not\nknow from where or by whom the for\ngeries had been supplied.\n\n\nPARIS, June ON. Ducret, editor of\nCocarde, which published the story con-\ncorning the documents stolen from the\nBritish embassy, and NJ. Norton. sup\nposed to be the forger of the same d0cu-\nments, were arrested to-day.\n\n\nErnest Vale presented in the Deputies\nto-day the report of the commission of\ninquiry into the Panama scandals. The\nreport exculpates from all blame M. de\nFreycinei. ex-Mlnister of War, and M.\nFloquet, ex-Presldent of the Chamber of\nDeputies. 1t also pronouuces false in\nevery particular the story that 104 Depu-\nties were bought with Panama money.\n\n=== 5_1908-10-24_p2_sn90050307_00414212888_1908102401_0383.json\nThe Prescott daily news. · 1908-10-24\ncrops: None\nheadline: BOHEMIAN FOR NOVEMBER.\n---\nAn unusual number of short stories are printed in The Bo- hemian Magazine for November. They are all good, but conspic- uous among them in gripping in terest and dramatic force is one entitled \"An Anarchist o the Big Timber\" by Henry Oyen-a story that relates the lone fight of backwoodsman, who believed he was being outraged in his rights, against a powerful lum- Der corporation in Chicago An other story with decidedly humorous vein is that by Pro- ter Emerson ( Browne, entitled, From Them That Have Vent\" this tells how ''Corrigan\" put his faith in mediums and of what befell. Other articles, are ''Twen- ty Thousand Miles with Bem- hardt.\" \"The Ghost of Science Latest methods of Craft\" etc.\n\n The portfolio of actresses of the present theatrical season which is printed in color in The Bohemian this month is especial ly attractive. The humorous de- partment as usual is full of new and witty things and is the best department of it's kind we have yet seen\n\n=== 44_1868-10-26_pna_sn84024738_00271741959_1868102601_0410.json\nThe daily dispatch. [volume] (Richmond [Va.]) 1850-1884 · 1868-10-26\ncrops: None\nheadline: LOCAL MATTERS.\n---\n\n\n\nINTERNAL REVENUE TAx.-The follow.\ning will show the amount of taxes assessed\non tobacco which has been shipped, in\nbond, from Richmond for each month since\nthe lst Of January, 1863, January, $751;\nO17.23; February, s67,062.90;= March\ns91, ,3S65O; April. $l78,5S7.95;, May.\ns349 ,741. go; June, s3s2,5O1; July, 1884,\n1888; August, 1921 ,O96.36 September,\nFsl59, ,9s6.72-making a total Of $685.\n5l2.79. .... Il. - s.1-55, est ~\"\nDuring the month of September there\nwas collected at the Office of the internal\nrevenue in this city 73,9l3.7S. OF this\namount there was collected on distilled\nspirits, at fifty cents per gallon, $40, ,49O.66\non manufactured tobacco, at thirty-twc\ncents tax, $S,925.46; and on smoking to-\nbacco, at sixteen cents tax, 81, ,l60.58. ana\n\n=== 3_1905-06-09_p1_sn86063592_00271764935_1905060901_0561.json\nLas Vegas daily optic. [volume] (Las Vegas, N.M.) 1880-1908 · 1905-06-09\ncrops: None\nheadline: The Whole Trail at Portland For $500\n---\nStrangely CARRIED people are seen on\nall sides. now a diminutive donkey\nbelabored by a boy in flowing robe\ndashes past, or a monstrous camel\nloaded with human freight lmnbers\nthrough the street. Ferocious looking\n| warriors dance about in mock combat.\nThe shrieking strains Of curious Oi-\nentail instruments are waited from the\ntheatre on the soft Oregon ozone.\nFor twenty-five cents the visitor gains\nentrance to this foreign show house\nand spends a pleasant half hour view\ning the antics of a strange people.\n\n\nA wonderful show is located almost\nOpposite the streets of Cairo, the in\nliant i%ncubators. There is no gaiety\nabout this concession. 1t represents\nthe most astonishing achievement of\nmodern science, the n%urturing OF the\nhuman being by artificial means. In\nthe pleasing little building housing\nthis attraction are several ingeniously\nCONSTRUCTED i%ncubators containing real\nlive infants. Visitors are allowed to\ngaze at them through plate glass, and\napparently the tots are content to re-\n\n\nmain in their snug homes. The ad-\n\n\nmission to the infant %incubators is\n\n\n25 cents.\n\n\nAnd SO on down the Trail the visitor\n\n\nmay meander. A great many of the\n\n\nshows offer admissions as low as III\n\n\ncents. The visitor finds foolish shows\n\n\nand serious shows, or he may devote\n\n\nhis time to instructive shows. No\nmatter how hard he hits the Trail\n\n\nhe cannot spend more than seven dol\n\n\nlars, which will admit him to thirty\n\n\nfive attractions, unless he chooses tc\n\n\ntake in a show more than once. The\n|entire list OF concessions, with theil\n\n\nprice of admission, follows\n\n\nGay Paree, Is cents; Concert hall\n\n\n25c; Infant in%cubators, 25c; Cascade\n\n\ngardens. csc; White Swan, 10c; Jap\n\n\nanese village and theatre, Csc: Div\nling Elks, Csc; New York ANNA\nshow. csc; Haunted swing, 25c\nStreets of Cairo, 15c; Theatre. 25c\nDonkey ride 25c; Camel ride soc; car\nnival OF Venice, soc; Land of Mid\n: might Sun, CSC; Tellevue, csc: Daven\nport farm, csc; Sistine Madonna, 10c\n} Trip to Niagara Falls, CSC: Galveston\n, Flood. csc; California Radium Exh'\n\n\nbit. 10c: Burns Cottage. 10c; Shootin\n: Gallery. 10c; Mirror Maze. 10c; Whit\n, Slave or Glimpse of the Harem, 10c\n/ Grand Siberian Railway, csc; an\nRlne G0tto. 25c.\n\n=== 14_1907-10-12_p1_sn93061711_00280773712_1907101201_0295.json\nThe Tucumcari news and Tucumcari times. [volume] (Tucumcari, N.M.) 1907-1921 · 1907-10-12\ncrops: None\nheadline: Ghost Sale and Fish Pond\n---\nEverybody is invited to attend\nthe box supper at Revuelno Sta.\nton next Wednesday night, Oct\nr. All ladies' bring supper for\n,vo, The funds collected will be\npsed in the completion Of uhe\nschool building,\n\n=== 6_1901-10-18_p6_sn83045462_00280655430_1901101801_0356.json\nEvening star. · 1901-10-18\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nsociations were rejected, and the conven-\ntion voted to admit hereafter a delegate\nfor each fifty members of the subordinate\n\n\nUnions\n\n\nWith the consideration of several amend\nments to the bylaws the morning session\nclosed. The delegates did not visit the\n\n\nWhite House but will instead send com\n\n\nmittee of two members to confer with the\n\n\nPresident tomorrow at noon.\n\n\nThe Closing Sessions.\n\n\nThe discussions during the morning ses-\nsions were keen and earnest. The after\n\n\nneon is to include the election of officers.\n\n\nIn the evening at the closing meeting the\n\n\ndelegates will be given test exhibitions.\n\n\nThe mediums chosen are Mrs. May S. Pep-\n\n\nper, whose letter tests of Wednesday ex\ncited the wonder of the non-spiritualists\n\n\npresent. and Miss Margaret Gauie whose\n\n\ntests tpnight promise to be entertaining\nas those of Mrs. Pepper Only one address\n\n\nis to be delivered.\n\n\nOi Conferences Held.\nA conference on the local societies occu-\npied the delegates the greater part of yes-\nterday afternoon. Chairman Gates of\nspecial committee on reports from the 'slo-\ncalls made statement to the convention\n| in which he deplored forcibly the neglect\n| OF many delegates to provide the national\nassociation with statements of their assets\nand membership. In reply considerable\n| group of the de1inquents made their way to\nre-\n| quired blanks Mr. Schrm made an an\n| nouncement which met with the most en-\nthuSiaStic favor, to the effect that a leg\n| acy to the Baltimore Union which had ,een\ncontested in the courts had been so ad-\njusted by compromise as to give the cause\n| OF spiritualism the sum of over $25 900, and\nwith that sum the congregation intended\n| to erect a modest hall and create an IN\n| dowment fund.\n\n\n! An address by Mr.. J. b. Towns md\n| former law partner to the late Senator C.\n8. Brice of Ohio, was on the program for\n| the close of the afternoon meeting. but\n| consumed nearly all of the session,\n! The chief features of the evening session\nwere the spirit readings of Mrs Zaii.l\n| Brown kates and Miss Margaret Ga,,Ie\n| Both mediums have methods similar 10\nthose used by most exponents of the spir-\n| itualistic faith. There were addresses Of\nthirty minutes each during the evening by\nMr. Thomas Grimshaw, an English stud.n,\nof psychic phenomena, and Mrs Helen p\nRussegue, one of the most argumen.atira\n| of the delegates on the rolls of the present\n| convention.\n\n\n| In response to the request of Mrs Sarah\nE. Musser that a tree in front of 2Oa9 T.\n| street northwest be removed the District\nCommissioners have replied that 1t stands\non private property. and therefore they are\nunable to undertake its. removal\n\n=== 33_1888-04-05_p1_sn92051501_00271762665_1888040501_0409.json\nThe Press. · 1888-04-05\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nThe Conn. Spiritualists' Association at its annual meeting in Hartford last week elected Mrs. M. A. Dwight of Stafford one of it's vice presidents The president is Geo. w. Burnham, and the secretary and treasurer Mrs. J. c. Robinson, both of Willimantic.\n\n=== 3_1875-10-25_p1_sn85033673_0028077943A_1875102501_1005.json\nPublic ledger. [volume] (Memphis, Tenn.) 1865-1893 · 1875-10-25\ncrops: None\nheadline: OBITUARY.\n---\nDied, on the morning Of the 23d inst,\nGILMER. son OF George Il, and Louisa Dabney,\nat their residence in DeSoto county, Miss.\naged twelve years.\n\n\nThe dread pcourge Of the country, swamp\nfever, seized upon this interesting little boy\nand soon hurried his spirit into the unknown\nworld. Not only was he the sweet treasure Of\nhis own family, but beloved by all who knew\nhim. Sweet tempered, amiable and sprlghtly.\nhe made himself interesting to strangers as\nwe'll as friends. When the shadows of death\nencompassed him, inspired by the affectionate\nimpulses of his heart, he called together his\nfather, his mother and brothers, and asked\neach one to kiss him before his going into the\nspirit land. One brother. when kissing him.\ntold him he would soon get we'll and they\nwould take the hunt together they had talked\nabout. mo, Ned\" he replied. II believe\nl would rather 90 to neavenl\" AnS snre\nenough, a few hours afterward, Inc gallant\nsoul Of tae little Christian soldier did start on\nits journey to Heaven, Comfort to the bro\nken-hearted mother She need not be told to\nremember little GILMER. His image and tho\nmemories Of his sayings and doings viii be\nenshrined in her heart of hearts, and will\ncast a sad shadow over all her future life.\nsee in thy gentle eyes a tear;\n\n\nlney turn TO me. il soFrowful thought;\nInou thinkest OF friends. the good and dear\n/, Who were for a time. aud now are not.\nLike these fair children Of cloud and past\n\n\nmat blisten 2 moment and then Are lost.\n\n\nFlake after fake\n\n\nAll lost In the dark and silent lake\" O.\n\n=== 10_1912-10-25_pna_sn93055779_0029587269A_1912102501_0809.json\nThe Cairo bulletin. (Cairo, Ill.) 1???-1928 · 1912-10-25\ncrops: None\nheadline: EXplairs Spiritualism\n---\nRev. c. H. Burks. OF Los Angeles,\nCalf, trance speaker, missionary-at-\nlarge of the National Splritualist\nassociation, will lecture Sunday ev-\nening at Odd Fellows hail az 7:30\no'clock He will answer some OF the\npopul1r objections raised against\nSpiritualism. Messages and tests\nafter the lecture The public most\ncordially invited to be present\n\n=== 10_1919-01-06_pna_sn86072054_00415660479_1919010601_0050.json\nThe West Virginian. [volume] (Fairmont, W. Va.) 1914-1974 · 1919-01-06\ncrops: None\nheadline: BLOWN FROM BED\n\n\nBY GAS EXPLOSION\n\nInterior Of Peter Audin's\nHome Much Disturbed\n\n\nBy the Effect.\n---\nA gas explosion which occurred in\nthe home of Peter Audin, glass\nworker. at 719 Virginia avenue, on\nSunday morning at 12:45 O'Clock did\nsiderable damage to the property\nand resulted in Mr.. Audin receiving\nsevere bruises about the body.\n\n\nThe explosion was caused, it Is be-\nlievd, by leak in the connection in\nthe kitchen range which ignited from\nthe flames in the stove. Little dam\nage was done from flames, but the\nconclusion caused by the explosion\nthrew Mr.. Audin from his bed and\nbroke windows, pictures and other\nbrio a brac. knocking plastering from\nthe walls and completely demolishing\nthe range. The house was rendered\nuninhabitable by the conclusion.\n\n\nThe fire department was called to\nthe scene and had no difficulty in ex-\ntinguishing the fire. The only dam\nage done by the flames was to lase\ncurtains, table covers and other high\nly inflammable materials. which were\nscorched and ruined by the fare up\nfrom the explosion.\n\n\nThe department was called GU. at\n12:45 o'clock Sunday afternoon to the\nhomo Of harles Grottendick at Ninth\nstreet :,Dd CASTOR avenue to extln\nguiyl. fines which had been caused\nFy a fire caused by a defective ilue.\nTic FIRE was soon extinguisheG\n\n\nl'ha first Q ia series OF tie fires\nwhich occurred within few haltrs\nwas q fir. from an overheated Kinney\nat the home Of George Graham on\nMarket street This, fire occurred at\n1870 Sunday morning and the East\nSide department responded to tie call\nand put cut the flames before they\nhad caused much damage.\n\n=== 106_1909-07-27_pna_sn84026749_100492465_1909072701_0292.json\nThe Washington times. [volume] (Washington [D.C.]) 1902-1939 · 1909-07-27\ncrops: None\nheadline: PALMISTS.\n\nSPIRITUALISM.\n---\nNINE. CORNISH, GIFTED MEDIUM reads\nyour life correctly; gives names, facts daily\nand Sunday. 601 Sd ST ~ E. cor. +, near\nUnion Station. ladies' only. jyl-u\n\n=== 4_1879-06-23_pna_sn86053573_0021110238A_1879062301_0606.json\nNational Republican. (Washington City (D.C.)) 1872-1888 · 1879-06-23\ncrops: None\nheadline: HOUSE REpR Els ryTa 7rYEs.\n---\n\n\n\nMr.. COBB, rising to question of personal priv\niege, sent to the Clerks desk and had read an OK\ntract from the indianapolis journal of the 18th\ninst, charging him with having repeated thread.\nbare lie about Massachusetts, regiment interfering\nwith voters in indianapolis in October, 1864. lie\nalso had read extracts from other papers and letters\nreceived by him in corroboration Of the statement\nwhich he had made in his speech, and again re.\niterated the charge that the election referred to had\nbeen nterfered with by the Massachusetts, regl\nment.\n\n\nMr.. WARNER, who had been stationed at Indian.\nnpols in October 1861, stated that It had officially\ncome TO his knowledge there had been Interference\nut the election.\n\n\nMr.. BROWNE denied the truth of the statement\nmade by his colleague. The reason why the Mas\nsGchnsets regiment had been quartered at Indian\napols was that conspiracy had heen uncalhed\nhaving as It's object the snbyersion of the State gov\nernmcnt and the turning II over TO the Confeder.\nncy. He did not charge that il was Democratic\ncoispiIacy but al the persons who Ind been tried\ngle Republican had been implicated In It.\n\n\nMr.. COBB asserted That Republicans had been\nconnected with that conspiracy.\n\n\nMr.. BAKER (lnd.) also denied Mr.. Cobb's state.\nment, and alluded TO the connection of Tile Demo.\ncratic pnrty with the knights of the Golden Circle\nlie regretted to sec that his friend from Ohio Mr.\nWnIheT had SO iar forgotten the lofty inspirations\nwith which Inc had been mstigated in iSGi that\nnow, in order to get himself on terms Of good fel.\nlowshlp with those whom he had fought against\nhe had got to hurl his darts over his shoulder at\nthe men whom he had then commanded.\nog M Nv Ia MhTYE uttered hS earnest protest\nyears to the war--three earnest years file had been\nfighting the ghost ever since. He despiscd\nmd loathed this fighting OF ghosts. lAppause on\nMr. WARNER after a great deal OF confusion, oh\ntanned the ioor in order to reply to the remarks of\nMr. Baker He repudated the statement that he\nhe stood IN 1851\n\n\nMr. iAKER thought that the gentleman from Ohio\nin tho condition of IL nDlitpnl Rim ink\nwho had gone 10 sleep OH the vanguard of liberty\nIn the ranks OF the Republican party and had\nwalked on the other side. lJeers on the Democratic\nside.I\n\n\nThe matter was then Dropped\n\n\nThe House then resumed In the morning hour\nthe consideration Of the bill prohibiting political\nassessments. =\n\n\nMr., IIosrETLEE withdrew the demand for the\nprevious question, moved TO refer the bill to the\nCommittee of the Whole, aud ON that motion de.\nmnnded the previous question.\n\n\nThe Republican,, then Notwithstanding an init,\nnation from Mr.. Springer that the bill would not\nbe taken up for consideration ~ the present session\nIelrnined from voting, thus leaving the House\nwithout quorum.\n\n\nDuring one of the roll.cRlls message was re-\nceivel from the PRESIDENT (at three y. M unnounc\ning MS approval OF the legislative bill. The an.\nnonmoemcnt was greeted with applause on the Re\npublican sdei amt Mr.. Garfield remarked \"WC\nhave provided for ourselves now let us provide fol\nthe rest Of the GOycmmcnt.\", ,\n\n\nThe morning hour expired without any actlo,\nOH the nnti-pOticnl assessment bin and II wen\nover till the ncNt morning hour.\n\n\nDuring the days session there was an utter\napathy manifested by mclnbeTs, 50 that IL wa,\nALMOST impossible 10 obtain quorum on any vote\nalthough OH one OF the loll-calls le9 members an\nEWcrGd.\n\n\nAlta qusrtcrpGst three the House went TO bust\nness on the Speakers table and disposed thereo\nMS follows.\n\n\nBIZ to establish post roads in certain States\nPassed.\n\n\neenUte amendments 10 bill relating TO printing\nimpressions from portraits or yigBelies of the Bu\nTeau Of Engraving ana Printing. Referred tC\nCommittee on banking and Currency.\n\n\nSenate amendments 10 House bin for the ap\npoinlincW of Mississippi River commission.\n\n\nAfter a debate, IN which Mr. baker opposed The\nbill us the Introduction Of gigantic scheme thN\nwould cost slOO,WOD0J anu IN which Mr.. GARFIELD\nsustained u as great national work IN which moi\nfrom tie North ana from the South Could ClaSJ\nhands together tho amendments were COnGurrCt\nnl-lVB ~ II, The principal amendment IL 10 max,\nthe number OF Commissioners seven, and is wal\nannounced by Nr. Hooker that Professor lIilgaIC\nIs 70 be the additional Commissioner,\n\n\nThe House then, at 1880 adjourned.\n\n=== 9_1884-01-11_pna_sn86072143_00280777079_1884011101_0017.json\nBurlington weekly free press. [volume] (Burlington, Vt.) 1866-1928 · 1884-01-11\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\n81 see. young gentleman, you wish to\ndraw back\"\n\n\n81 draw back /\" exclaimed the student.\n\"Well, if Ihad fifty guiueas you should\nsee whether l wish to do So\"\n\n\n\"Herc,\" suid !, \"arc four guineas which\nwill stake on your wager\"\n\n\nNo sooner had made this proposition\nthan the rest of the company, attracted\nNY the singularity of the affair. came for\nward to lay down their money, and in u\nminute Or two the fifty guineas were sub\nscribed.\nThe gentleman was so sure OF winning\nthat he placed all tho money in the stu\ndents hands and prepared for tho expert\nment.\n\n\nWe selected for the purpose small\nsummer house in the garden, perfectly\nisolated, and having no means of exit but\na door aud n window, which wo carefully\nfastened, after placing the young man\nwithin, together with writing materials on\na small table.\nWe remained outside with tho gentle\nman amongst u.s.\n\n\nIn a slow, solemn manner, he began to\nchant the following lines ;\nwhat rsetn slow from the oceau caves and the\n\n\n\n\n\nThe phantom pGlc sets his backcncd foo. on tic\nfresh green turf.\n\n\nThen raising his voice, lie said\n\n\n''You asked to see your friend, Francis\nVilliers, who was drowned three years\nago, off tho const Of South America\nwhat do you see?'\n\n\n81 see,' replied tho student, \"a white\nlight arising near the window but it has\nno form\"\n\n\nWe (the spoctators) remained profound-\nu silent.\n\n\ncare you afraid p asked the grntle.\nman.\n\n\n~ am not, replied the student, firmly.\nIhen, after moments pause, the elder\ncommenced by three stamps on the\nground, muttering\nAnd the phantom white whose clay-cold face was\nonce So fair.\n\n\nDries vi h his shroul his clingng yes and his sea-\ntossed hair.\nOnce mole the solemn question\n\n\n\"You, who would see revealed the mys-\nteries of tho tomb-what do You zee\nnow p\n| The answer came:\nis covered with long veil--it stands\nstill T.\n\n\ncare you airaid?'\n\n\n81 am not\n\n\nWith strange and frantic gestures the\ngentleman then chantod\nAnd the phantom 50 d to the smoking socr, come\nfOm he SOUTH,\n\n\nPit tiny hand On my htnd-thy hoar' IN NV heart\n| thy mouth on my mouth\n\n\n\"What do you see ?\" he asked.\n| ola pppps-ba r,,1rsi,gc ,,,o bo :,\nstretching ont hand-'tis Francis vllliers\n-he will have me Help, help | Save\nme -\n\n\ncare you afraid now?\" asked the other\nin n mocking voice\n\n\nA piercing cry, then a stifled groan, are\nthe only replies given to the question.\n\n\n\"Hslp that rash youth, said the gon-\nman, walking away rapidly ''let him\nkeep his money and be wiser in the fu\nture.\n\n\nWe opened the door of the summer\nhouse and found the student in convul\nsions. A paper signed \"Francis Villiers'\nWAS lying on the table.\n\n\nAs soon as the students senses were re-\nstored he asked, vehemently, where was\nthat vile sorcerer-he would kill him HI\nsought him throughout the inn then\nwith the speed of q mailman, he startet\nacross the fields in pursuit of him.\n\n\nThe dark faced man paused, as if hi.\nstory was concluded, and took long pull\nat his tumbler of punch.\n\n\n\"Well ?\" said I.\n\n\n\"Well O. said he, nonchantly.\n\n\n\"There is something more, isn't there\nThe story is not quite ended ?\"\n\n\nQuite\" said he.\n\n\nIL don't understand. How did i\nend p\n\n\nEND p cried he. 1T did not end\nNeither of them ever turned up. The,\nhad simply humbugged us out of on\nmoney by u villainous dodge. I told 20,\nthe Cork people were artlcss and iunocen\nvery\"\n\n=== 12_1875-05-18_p1_sn85026936_00393348628_1875051801_0471.json\nNone · 1875-05-18\ncrops: None\nheadline: \n---\nipproen If, Die scgenieitigen seI6aftli-\nden I~erbinoun9en, ipsld)e 5mil6en ben\nYer. Staaten uno &robbritionien bes\nlteben uno Der &ei6tbum an M~ineralien\nuno Walerfroft Die Nirginia Bat, Iowie\nDie 9initiSe Gage fir 6abrifen uno Sn-\nonltric, etc. etc. (2eioer nur iuenn elm\nSremoer fomnlt uro cine GDaierfraft\nfanfen min, miro Elm iol9er 9reis sefor=\nbert, oab er lieber Iein @eIo bebdlt uub\nmetter 5iebt; pie mir es 9e6abt 1aben.)\nIS beiBt: rSelpnoers bietet 1irginia\nSortbetIe fttr briti d EMANCIPATION\nweIGe ni6t iibertrohen werocn fonner,\n6eHn es lit fem 2anb auf groen> tueI6es\n\n\nlid better fir Die Galtlr Der Srauben\nuno anoere Gr'iGte sisnet\". (g8ir ia\n\n\nben ME gcbDrt, ooB Die 8riten aiobe\nWelnbausf fino.) Na6oem Der Sdrei-\nDer nod peitcr iiber bas ausgcaeidnete\n9anO bier, wp alts better 9e6eibt, sc=\nfa9t fabrt er fort: vgs lit ,pabr, bab,\nmas asrifnltur betrift, Wir9jnia peit\nbtnter benz Nnroen uno @eIteu auriid=\niteDt, ober @ues mas febIt, nm GO au Dem\nbeittn @sricultur Staot Der Union au\nMAIDEN, 1T capital uno cine sel6idte\nTertpoltun9, pie es in ben nOrbli6en Ii.\n. eltll9en Staaten gu finben it,\" (9Der\nllno Sleleni9ert, pelde Den Norben uno\nnamentliA Den @eiten reid semabt 6a=\n\n\nben! Wenn li9 Der 8eriaIler oes Dir\ntifels etlpas erfunoigen mD@te, uiroe er\nfinher, oob es buuptld0lib bie9 eutidea\nioarer, ober nidt Die Yritsn.) 6errer\nbeibt es: ySn n 3ueise Der 2ano\nwlrtbldaft lollte 8irsiuia ollie anoeren\nStoaten Der Union ijberbicten. ndmlih\nin Der S6aafSudt. (Gs lit mabr, bic\nsSole 9eoelbt bier ,ett gut, DEFENDERS\nDie f6parSe.) Sir Die @rseusune uno\nNerarbeitung Der Wo0e lino Sic gno=\nldnoer mebr seeisiet als irseno elm ara\noeres WoIt Der Groe.\" Ser @rtitel\nidlieBt nit ben @orten: uGnelilbe @a\npltolllten 