ed in discussing the\nlarge amount Of money which the de-\nparted gentleman vas found TO have\nleft than IN dcplcring his taking away\nThe president of the railroad himself\nwas conspicuous among those present\nand was particularly affected by the\ndimensions of the fortune which the\ndecedent had amassed All through the\nfunereal function he continuously muir\nINJURED: where did he get it? Where\ndid he get it?' and when his ceremonial\nobligations were discharged he departed\nstill repeating increasingly: Where did\nhe get it? Where did he get it2'\n\n\nTraffic managers of great lines have\nbeen observed to prosper in ratio\nwholly disproportionate to their salaries\nIn fact. very many OF them have become\nexceedingly rich, matter which has\nnot altogether escaped criticism. Some\nof them have made money by going\nlong or short Of the stock OF the cor\nporation employing them, accordingly\nas their information and Judgment\nprompted and IN respect of THIS practice\nthe public censuro has never been severe\n1t is not unlikely that the course which\nMr.. ROOSEVELT has pursued toward the\nmanagement OF tine railroads may pro-\nwoke a more minute and attentive scru-\ntinv of the practices of their officials,\nin which case it may appear that the\nsources of their wealth were far more\nquestionable.\n\n\nWe venture to think that Mr.. RoosE-\nWELT * BY this time satisfied that the\nreally grave charge against the rail-\nroads of the country is not the high\nrates charged for transportation but\nthe giving of secret and criminal re-\nbates We have never INDUCED the\nbelief that rate cutting was inspired\nsolely by desire for increased tonnage.\nThe rebates and cut rates enjoyed by\nthe Standard Oil Company cannot be\nexplained solely by the competition\nbetween rival roads for the oil COM\npany's traffic. No one who is conversant\nwith the railroad business could be hood\nwinked into such a belief. There were\ncertain phenomena connected with the\ngrowth of that company which are,\nindeed. inscparableSfr the history Of\nall great corporations during the last\nquarter OF a century, but which have\nescaped general notice and have never\nbeen brought out into the light.\n\n\nIf President ROOSEVELT persists in\nthe direction which has led to the late\nrevelations touching the criminal man\nagement of the Atchison, Topeka and\nSanta Fe Railroad, some of the phenom-\nena will see the light beyond a doubt and\nif. in consequence, some Of our leading\nrailroad men find themselves in the penl-\nTertiary they will have only themselves\n10 blame, and the public may fifty con-\ngraduate itself. Then and in that\nevent there will no longer remain any\nillusions Us to the nature and causes Of\nrailroad acknowledgement, and they will\nbe found to have been at least as pref\njudicial to the interests Of the people who\nactually own the railroads as they have\nbeen detrimental TO The public welfare.\nThe profit and advantage of the secret\nrebate have not accrued solely 10 the\nCORPORATION for whose benefit it was\napparently created.\n\n\nThe late Mr. JAMES AICHENRY often\ncomplained 10 US that what he described\nas ghost trains were run upon the Erie\nRailroad, trains which performed their\ndue function upon the operating sched-\nules OF the road, but of which the results\nnever penetrated into the traffic returns.\nHad he lived longer and become ac-\nouainted with these later revelations of\nthe methods Of railroad management\nwe believe he would have hit upon a\nmore practical and adequate hypothesis.\nThere are two sides to the question, and\nboth are of grave Importance; and the\nplundering of the public has proceeded\npar passu with the defraudng Of the\nrailroad investor We are informed that\none railroad which freed itself from the\nrate cutting and rebate system showed\nan increase of earnings IN the first twelve\nmonths of its emancipation of something\nlike SIC. 600. $50 Certain Industries which\nwere involved were less profitably con-\nQuoted. perhaps. while ertainindividuals\nconcerned found their personal emolp-\nments subjected 10 a severe shrinkage.\n\n\nWe hope the Atchison scandal will be-\nurobed TO its uttermost depths and that\nthe President will let no guilty mar\nescape. We could wish that the duty\nof doing the necessary work dcvolvec\nupon a body Of better repute thar\nthe Interstate Commerce Commission\nwhich for incompetence and peryersitJ\nis without a peer and which enjoys\nin an equal degree the contempt ot\nthe courts and the indifference Of thu\nrailroads Perhaps, too, the President\nwill be convinced that in the pursuit\nof his amb ition he has found a weapon\nmore effective than Congress and less\ndoubtful than an appeal from the Con\nstitution. We mean public opinion. II,\nhas certainly enlisted it as it never wa.\nenlisted before, and we think that viii\nits aid anything that is desirable can b'\naccomplished. 1t were better far thai\nthe public continued to suffer for a time\nand that railroad owners were robbed %\nall they possess than that the principle\nand spirit of our theory OF govcramen\n\n\n5000 fro,, Vur., ,-reDus lun\n\n=== 1_1896-07-06_p14_sn85066387_0017503741A_1896070601_0113.json\nThe San Francisco call. · 1896-07-06\ncrops: None\nheadline: She Wants to Join Her Husband\nin the Spirit Land When\n\n\nUnder Them.\n\nIS NOW IN A PADDED CELL\n\nThe Doctors at the Receiving Hos.\npital Can See No Signs of In.\n\n\nsanity in Her.\n---\nThe case of Mrs. Allie Gender, widow\n53 years of age, will form subject for in\nvestigation by the Insanity Commission\ners this morning.\n\n\nShe is in padded cell in the Receiving\nHospital charged with insanity, but any\none who talks with her cannot see the\nslightest sign OF mental alteration in hel\nconduct or speech. Yet her friends say\nshe is crazy, and too dangerous to be al\nlarge.\n\n\nTwelve years ago her husband was killed\nin railroad accident. He had an accl-\ndent policy for $500, and this sum was\npaid to the widow. or rather to Albert\nShainwald, her brother-in-law, living at\n207 Geary street, who was administrator Of\nher husbands estate.\n\n\nThe widow was left with three young\nchildren, boy and two girls. They were\nput in home and the mother worked\nhard to support herself and them. The\n$500 was placed on deposit in the San\nFrancisco Savings Union bank and with\ninterest it has reached the sum of $2100. OF\nthis amount $500 has been withdrawn and\nloaned to G. Petrie, printer in Alameda.\n\n\nMrs. Gender about two years ago went\nto live with her brother-in-law, but three\nweeks ago she removed to 238 Sixth street,\nwhere she engaged a room. According TO\nthe landlady, Mrs. George, she showed\nsymptoms of insanity shortly after being\nin her house and on Saturday she became\nviolent and threatened to take her life, so\nDR.. Bohm was sent for and he ordered\nthat she be sent to the Receiving Hospital.\n\n\nHer chief hallucination is that her hus-\nbands spirit is constantly with her.\nWhen walking on the street she imagined\nhat har hnos do J., II\nverse with him as if he were in the flesh\nat night she would alarm every one in\nthe house by going from room to room\nsearching for her husband and when she\ncould not find him she became violent and\nhad to be placed under restraint.\n\n\nWhen she became calm she would talk\nnationally and say she wanted to Join her\nhusband in the spirit land and if the\nmoney was only divided among her chill\ndren she would soon be with him.\n\n\nThe Oldest, young man % years of age,\nis in Mendocino County, where he has\nbought some Government land. One of\nthe girls is married and the other is a\nservant with family on Union street.\nApparently the money is not to be di-\nvided till the youngest. who is now 20\n| years of age. attains the age of 2i.\n| Speaking of her arrest Mrs. Gender said\nyesterday: sl think it is all a Joke to\nkeep me here. was in my room vester.\nday when two men wearing blue uniforms\ncame and asked me to put on my shoe.\nand come with them. Then they put me\nin patrol wagon and brought me here\n| But | don't know why. l am not crazy\nand | cannot see why l should be kept\nhere\"\n\n\nThe doctors at the hospital who have\nconversed with her say that she does not\nexhibit the slightest symptoms of in.\nsanity, but the landlord and landlady o.\nthe lodging-house at 238 Sixth Street, DR.\nBohm and others say that she has spells\nand when under them she is violently\n/ insane.\n\n=== 30_1946-07-13_p2_sn83045462_00280604781_1946071301_0507.json\nEvening star. · 1946-07-13\ncrops: None\nheadline: 4o Seek Ghost Job\n---\nPROVIDENCE, R. I. (P).-Just 40\nmen passed civil service examina-\nton for motor vehicle inspector.\nbut they're holding onto their pres\nent jobs-if they have any. For\nthere is not single vacancy for\nthe post.\n\n=== 6_1914-07-25_pna_sn86061215_00295875410_1914072501_0441.json\nOttumwa tri-weekly courier. [volume] (Ottumwa, Iowa) 1903-1916 · 1914-07-25\ncrops: None\nheadline: ,cr1Aprrp xv-(Concluded.)\n---\nfCHApTER xv.-(Concluded)\n\n\nBut only for a minute; then they\ncaved. crumpled up, and took their\nmoney-perhaps because couple OF\nDAYS before they had been watching\nme tossing tomato cans into the ali\nnd,bouring them with my pistol, with\nfewer misses than looked good, to\nthem.\n\n\nThree months did fir the Double\nCircla. Pacif1ed, there was no further\nneed of my services. My happy little\nouting was over, and it was trail back\nto the home: back, with the scent OF\nlife In my nostrils and the thrill of\naction in my blood, into the scent or\nthe grave and the asphyxiating atmos-\nphere Of the mentally feeble and mort\nbund.\n\n\nBut. happily, the Double Circla Incl\ndent proved the dawning OF the bright\nest period Of my life, a dawning whose\nshining rays were shortly to lead me\ninto a harbor Of peace and Joy; OF con-\ntentment and bliss, that I had never\ndreamed could be mine.\n\n\nDown OF the home one day came\nfrom the redwood lumber camps OF\nnorth California an old soldier whom\n/ had known as a lad in Ionia County.\n\n\nThrough the war he had served IN\nMichigan regiment. Only twice since\nhad he revisited the old home. but he\nhad continued correspondence with his\nfamily.\n\n\nFrom him I learned-oh, Joy! the\ngreatest that could come to me-not\nonly that Tom Harper had recovered\nof the wound that had left me regard\ninR myself for half a century as an\noutlaw. not only that my mischievous\nBessie McVicker still lived, but that\nthrough all the weary years OF my ap'\nsence she had remained single!\n\n\nGould It be that she had withheld\nher heart and hand through all those\nyears for memory Of me? No. That\nwere indeed folly Of the widest.\n\n\nsha wait for me? She, who had\nnever passed me so much as kindly\nword. who was ever making me the\nsubject Of cruel jests! She, above all,\nwho had run to Toms side and taken\nhis head In her lap when, fallen under\nmy blow he lay so still In the snow!\n\n\nNonsense. Doubtless instead she\nhad forgotten the fact had ever ex-\nlisted. But would know; must\nknow\n\n\nPromptly her answer came. Fol\nlowed a correspondence in which she\nowned she had loved me from the first;\nthat she we'll deserved the sousing IN\nthe sap-trough had given her; and\nthat she had only run to Tom IN fear\nmy blow had killed him. and In the\nhope she might revive him and save\nme the grave consequences that must\nensue in case he died at my hands.\n\n\nBut oh! the grief intermingled 4\nour 10y! She remained as poor as |;\ncould no more come to me than could\n70 to her She was shackled by her\npoverty in the sugar-bush where had\nleft her, I by mine in the Soldiers\nHome. We were like two disembodied\nspirits reunited, denied the pressure OF\ndearly loved lips. the clasp Of quick\npulsing hands.\n\n\nBut, ali! the pride and happiness our\nletter intercourse brought me! Bessie\nhad stayed true to me as to her. And\ntome it was all the dearer that mine\nhad been a silent alteration had never\ndared express, and yet so strong she\nmust have sensed it, to have responded\nq plainly she had, by lifetime's de-\nvotion.\n\n\nTime passed-much time-until we\n\n=== 1_1897-06-30_p4_sn82015104_00280768765_1897063001_0257.json\nThe record-union. · 1897-06-30\ncrops: None\nheadline: FATHER AND SON.\n\nThey Are Having Trouble Over the\n\n\nLatter's Earnings.\n---\nWhat will probably prove an inter\nesting case will be tried before a Po-\nlice Court jury next Saturday morn\ning.\n\n\nComplaint was made at police head\nquarters on Saturday last, by the\nfather and brother of Pearley Chap\npell, that that young man had fallen\nunder the influence of an alleged spir-\nitualist, named Mrs. Quarles, and at\nher suggestion had refused to return\nto the paternal roof\n\n\nThe boys story is that his father\nand brother interfered to prevent him\nfrom drawing his salary and that he\nhas not been allowed fair share of\nhis earnings.\n\n\n1t was at first the intention of the\nelder Chappell to have his son Pear\nley, who is about IS years old, ar\nrested, but acting on subsequent ad-\nvice concluded to bring a charge of\ndisturbing the peace against Mrs.\nQuarles.\n\n\nYoung Chappell's brother, who is en-\nployed in Lavensons shoe store, has\nalso caused the arrest of Mrs. Clough,\na respectable woman who keeps a 1odg-\n","numLines":1486,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1486}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a81f8a23-cae3-4841-bc96-e989bbbab05b","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"other"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"phase-2c-reader","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
